---
import Base from "../layouts/Base.astro";
import Section from "../components/Section.astro";
---
<Base title="Mission and approach" description="Why ForBetterCommunity builds capacity rather than delivering aid, and how that choice shapes every project we support." hero={true}>
  <div slot="hero" class="max-w-3xl">
    <h1 class="text-3xl sm:text-4xl">Mission and approach</h1>
    <p class="mt-5 text-white/75 leading-relaxed">
      ForBetterCommunity helps communities in need become self-sufficient and environmentally
      sustainable through cooperation, skills and the tools required for long-term success.
    </p>
  </div>

  <Section kicker="The premise" title="Aid maintains. Capacity releases.">
    <div class="prose-fbc text-[16px]">
      <p>
        Relief has its place. After a hurricane or an earthquake, nothing replaces food, water and shelter
        delivered fast. But relief that becomes permanent stops being help. It creates a dependency that
        outlives the emergency, and it quietly tells a community that its future is administered elsewhere.
      </p>
      <p>
        We work the other way around. Every project we support is judged against a single question:
        what will still be standing here, and running, once we are gone? A cooperative that owns its land.
        An irrigation system the community maintains. A trade school that trains the next cohort without us.
        A family that produces its own electricity and its own vegetables.
      </p>
      <p>
        This is slower than distribution. It is harder to photograph. It is the only approach we have seen
        change what a place is capable of.
      </p>

      <h2>Work in return</h2>
      <p>
        This is the principle that shapes everything else. Every time a family receives support from us,
        it gives a piece of work back to its own community. Not as a condition imposed from outside, but
        so that what arrives is never a handout. It is earned, and it belongs to the people who earned it.
      </p>
      <p>
        The difference shows up years later. A community that was given something waits for the next gift.
        A community that built something knows it can build the next one.
      </p>

      <h2>Where we work</h2>
      <p>
        Central America and the Caribbean, and within those regions, small communities rather than capitals.
        In a village, a well-placed intervention moves the whole population. The same effort in a city
        disappears into the scale of the problem.
      </p>

      <h2>What we bring, and what we do not</h2>
      <p>
        We instigate, we guide and we facilitate. We do not own the projects, we do not run them from abroad,
        and we do not staff them permanently. Ownership sits with the community from the first day, because a
        project the community does not own is a project it will not maintain.
      </p>

      <h2>Two entities, one mission</h2>
      <p>
        ForBetterCommunity operates through two independent legal organizations. This entity,
        ForBetterCommunity USA, is a 501(c)(3) nonprofit corporation incorporated in Montana and oversees
        work in Honduras and Guatemala.
        <a href="https://canada.forbettercommunity.org">ForBetterCommunity Canada</a>
        is a separate federally incorporated nonprofit that carries the work in Cuba.
        Separate boards, separate accounts, separate obligations. Nothing crosses between them.
      </p>
    </div>
  </Section>
</Base>
